+# Independent analytic reconstruction (before prior reports)
+
+Claim: for fixed H in (1/4,1), independent standard equal-H fBm components, X=int B1 dB2 in the canonical polygonal convention, both full uniform grids, every L2 measurable F, every n>=1 and T>0, ||X-F||2 >= c_H T^(2H)n^(1/2-2H). This is neither a sharp result nor a claim of novelty, endpoints, uniform H control, adaptive or nonuniform sampling, unequal H, or dependence.
+
+Family mechanism: real Gaussian spectral covariance paths localized between observations; a quadratic cross-component witness and a simultaneous upper Gram bound. Evidence below is analytic deduction; canonical area existence is a primary-source dependency to be checked. Remaining source/implementation/custody gaps are independent of the algebra.
+
+1. Spectral normalization. D=integral |exp(i xi)-1|²|xi|^(-1-2H) is finite by powers 1-2H at zero and -1-2H at infinity, and positive. K_t=D^(-1/2)(exp(it xi)-1)|xi|^(-H-1/2) is Hermitian symmetric. The real subspace of complex L2 has a real inner product because the imaginary integrand is odd. Norm increments equal |t-s|^(2H), and polarization gives exactly the standard covariance. Independent real isonormal processes supply independent components. An auxiliary direction need not be path-measurable; transfer is by joint law of finite sums/observations and L2 convergence.
+
+2. Fourier sign and regularity. With hhat=int exp(-it xi)h(t)dt, u_h=sqrt(D)/(2pi)|xi|^(H+1/2)conj(hhat). Conjugating u in <K,u> yields hhat and hence h(t)-h(0), without reflected arguments. Reality of h makes u Hermitian symmetric. phi=x^4(1-x)^4, f=phi' and g=phi have zero traces of r,r',r'' at the endpoints. Zero extensions belong to W^(3,1); f''' may jump but contains no distributional atom. Integration by parts gives |rhat| <= min(L_r,N_r|xi|^-3). Absolute inversion and weighted L2 integrability follow for alpha=2H+1 in (3/2,3): the weighted tail exponent alpha-6<-3, the origin exponent alpha>0. Direct polynomial integration gives a=int(phi')²=4/45045>0. Norm constants will receive an exact independent polynomial control.
+
+3. All-frequency Gram bound. r_j(t)=Delta^H r(t/Delta-j) has transform Delta^(H+1)exp(-ij Delta xi)rhat(Delta xi). The u inner product has exp(i(j-k)eta); the scale exponent is 2H+2-alpha-1=0. Therefore its symbol is D/(4pi²) sum_l |theta+2pi l|^alpha |rhat(theta+2pi l)|². The zero translate is <=pi^alpha L²; each nonzero translate uses distance >=pi(2|l|-1) and the third-derivative estimate. Since alpha-6<0, the odd decreasing-series bound is 1+1/[2(5-alpha)]. Tonelli applies to a nonnegative product with the finite trigonometric-polynomial modulus squared. Parseval supplies 2pi sum b_j², proving A<=M_f I, C<=M_g I with M_r=D Pbar_r/(2pi). This is an upper bound, not rough increment coercivity: the increment all-ones variance is n^(2H), so a uniform positive lower multiple of n is impossible when H<1/2.
+
+4. Invisible witness. f_j and g_j vanish at all grid times (also 0,T). Each U_j=W1(u_fj), V_j=W2(u_gj) has zero covariance with all grid observations. Joint Gaussianity makes the whole auxiliary vector independent of the whole observation vector. Y=sum U_j V_j is centered and independent of the observation sigma-field; EFY=0 for any L2 observation-measurable F, including nonlinear ones. Cross-component independence gives EY²=sum_jk A_jk C_jk=tr(AC). PSD order gives tr(AC)<=M_f tr(C)<=nM_fM_g via C^(1/2)(M_f I-A)C^(1/2), with no commutation or off-diagonal sign assumption.
+
+5. Pairing at fixed n. For every finite Euler sum, independence factors E[B1_left U_j]E[Delta B2 V_j], giving exactly sum f_j(left)Delta g_j. For fixed T,n,j, L2 convergence of Euler sums and U_jV_j in L2 pass the left pairing to X. Deterministic smooth Riemann sums converge to int f_j g_j'=Delta^(2H)a. Summing only finitely many cells gives EXY=n Delta^(2H)a. Cauchy-Schwarz gives error >=a sqrt(n)Delta^(2H)/sqrt(M_f M_g)=2pi a/[D sqrt(Pbar_f Pbar_g)] T^(2H)n^(1/2-2H). Positive pairing also ensures nonzero witness variance.
+
+6. Area-limit bridge. Let I_j be the area on a unit cell relative to its left endpoint. Chen, stationary increments, and joint self-similarity give E|X-L_m|²=T^(4H)m^(-4H)[m c0+2(m-1)c1+2 sum_(r=2)^(m-1)(m-r)c_r]. Existence gives finite c0 and |c1|<=c0. For separated cells differentiated covariance is smooth, so component independence gives c_r=H²(2H-1)² times the integral over two unit triangles of |u-v|^(2H-2)|s-t|^(2H-2). It is nonnegative and <=H²(2H-1)²(r-1)^(4H-4)/4. No singular diagonal or adjacent-cell density is being integrated. Thus mean-square convergence follows from m^(1-4H), m^-2(1+log m), and m^-2 in the three H regimes separated at 3/4. At H=1/2 use Ito isometry T²/(2m) separately.
+
+7. Polygon correction. Direct linear-segment integration yields P_m=L_m+(1/2)sum Delta B1 Delta B2. Its square expectation equals T^(4H)m^(-4H)/4 [m+2sum_(r=1)^(m-1)(m-r)rho(r)²], by independent components. The exact second difference has integral derivative bound |rho(r)|<=H|2H-1|(r-1)^(2H-2) for r>=2. The same three convergence regimes follow. Thus Euler and polygon limits coincide. At H=1/4 the correction diagonal alone is T/4, so this bridge does not extend; at H=1 standard fBm is linear and endpoint-recoverable and D diverges. n=1 is included without asymptotics. Brownian exact optimum is a prior claim requiring source confirmation; the witness constant is merely a lower bound. The antisymmetric halved area differs from X by an observed endpoint product and has the same residual, while the unhalved difference doubles it.
+
+8. Source-version test: if v1 discards every lag>=2, c2 is strictly positive (for H!=1/2) because both distances lie in [1,3], yielding c2>=H²(2H-1)²3^(4H-4)/4. Its 2(m-2) pairs persist at the rough normalized scale. A journal proof that keeps all fixed lags and discards only a moving tail avoids that specific error. This does not label the journal erroneous or certify its exact coefficients.
+
+Analytic reconstruction conclusion: no internal mathematical gap identified in this derivation; source dependencies and all package claims remain to be audited. Universal conclusions rest on the derivation, not finite computations. Completion estimate 18%.
